[LOCAL SECTION HEADING] 2.3. Crop Protection
[88, PDF page 11] tative management strategies and intervention methods for weed, disease and pest control [6,25,26].
[89, PDF page 11] Several important components of organic crop-health management regimes also affect the nutritional quality and safety of crops: these are described in separate sections/subsections below.
[90, PDF page 11] Specifically, organic crop protection is based on the following practices:
[91, PDF page 11] Use of diverse crop rotation (see Section 2.1 above);
[92, PDF page 11] Non-use of water-soluble mineral N and P-fertilizers (see Section 2.2 above);
[93, PDF page 11] Use of organic fertilizers and soil amendments to increase soil suppressiveness (see Section 2.3.1 below);
[94, PDF page 11] Use of environmental enrichment measures to increase populations of natural enemies of invertebrate pests on farms (see Section 2.3.2);
[95, PDF page 11] Use of elicitors for crop resistance where necessary (see Section 2.3.3 below);
[96, PDF page 11] Use of biological control products for pest and disease control where necessary (see Section 2.3.4 below);
[97, PDF page 11] Use of barrier, mating disruption, and mass-trapping methods for pest control where necessary (see Section 2.3.5 below);
[98, PDF page 12] Use of thermal weed control or soil disinfection; these practices are increasingly restricted and should only be used as a last resort (see Section 2.3.6 below);
[99, PDF page 12] Use of pesticide products that are permitted in organic farming; these products should only be used as a last resort (see Section 2.3.6 below);
[100, PDF page 12] Non-use of all synthetic chemical pesticides (see Section 2.3.7 below).
[101, PDF page 12] In contrast, crop protection in intensive conventional farming systems relies heavily on the use of synthetic chemical pesticides, which are prohibited in organic farming [ 4].
[102, PDF page 12] It is important to note that many management practices (e.g., monoculture and short rotations, zero and minimum tillage) used in conventional farming are dependent on regular pesticide applications [5,29,31,35,42,57].
[103, PDF page 12] Differences in the level of pesticide use between organic and conventional farming systems are thought to have the largest potential direct and indirect effects on food quality and safety, and human health [57].
[104, PDF page 12] The current knowledge about the effects of organic versus conventional crop protection protocols on (i) pesticide residues, and (ii) other nutritionally relevant compounds in food crops are therefore also described separately, in Section 2.3.7 below.

